    {
      "field_name": "diagnostic_criteria",
      "value": "massive painful rigidity and superimposed spasms in the extremities, electromyography showed continuous co-contraction of flexors and extensors in the extremities, immunohistochemistry clearly showed the expression of estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor in the cancer cells, only anti-amphiphysin autoantibodies were present at a high titer in the patient’s serum on the Mayo Clinic paraneoplastic autoantibodies test",
      "quote": "A 35-year-old woman with massive painful rigidity and superimposed spasms in the extremities was admitted to our hospital (Osaka Medical College, Osaka, Japan). Her right axillary lymph nodes were as swollen as a tip of a thumb. The biopsy assay from a right axillary lymph node showed a ductal metastatic carcinoma. Furthermore, immunohistochemistry clearly showed the expression of estrogen receptor and progesterone receptor in the cancer cells (Figure 1). Curiously, no origin was detected in her breast by imaging analysis, including mammography, ultrasonography, computed tomography and positron emission tomography–computed tomography. Therefore, we considered that she suffered from occult breast cancer. Interestingly, further assessment of immunohistochemis - try showed the massive expression of amphiphysin in the cancer (Figure 1). Furthermore, only anti-amphiphysin autoantibodies were present at a high titer in the patient’s serum on the Mayo Clinic paraneoplastic autoantibodies test, and Hu, Ri, ANNA-3, Ma2, PCA-1, PCA-2, PCA-Tr, CRMP-5, amphiphysin, PQ/- and N-type VGCC, VGKC, nicotinic acetylcholine receptors, and GAD65 screening. Furthermore, electromyography showed continuous co-contraction of flexors and extensors in the extremities.",
      "confidence": "high"
    },
